// Generated by scripts/generate-port-catalog.py. Do not edit by hand.
//
// Numbering conventions (from the source's $doc):
//   Per-chassis port-location catalogue. Keyed by `sysctl hw.model` (the chassis identifier the
//   kernel exposes — e.g. `Mac14,12`). Each `ports` entry maps a (connector, port_number) pair to a
//   human-friendly chassis location. Port numbering follows what
//   `AppleHPMInterfaceType10.PortNumber` (USB-C / USB-A / HDMI) and `IOPort.PortNumber` (USB-A)
//   publish. When the kernel's port number doesn't have a physical-chassis meaning (HDMI, MagSafe,
//   SDXC — never more than one per chassis), the entry uses `port_number: 1`. Capability strings
//   mirror Apple's tech-spec wording — `Thunderbolt / USB 4` (M1/M2/M3 base), `Thunderbolt 4`
//   (Pro/Max chassis pre-M4), `Thunderbolt 5` (M4 Pro/Max). See CLAUDE.md → 'Adding a new Mac model
//   to the port-location catalogue' for the procedure when Apple ships a new chassis.
//
// MacBook Pro chassis note (from the source's $mbp_chassis_note):
//   The 2021+ MacBook Pro Pro/Max chassis is identical across every generation from M1 Pro through
//   M5 Max. LEFT side (rear → front): MagSafe 3, USB-C (kernel port 1 — right next to MagSafe),
//   USB-C (kernel port 2), 3.5 mm headphone jack. RIGHT side (rear → front): HDMI, USB-C (kernel
//   port 3 — the lone right-side TB, sandwiched between HDMI and the SD slot), SDXC card slot. The
//   kernel numbers the left-side pair first because they share an HPM chip; the right-side lone port
//   is on its own HPM. The M3 base (Mac15,3) is the only chassis that trims this layout, dropping
//   the lone right-side TB so its kernel ports 1/2 are the only USB-Cs on the chassis.
//
// 59 models, 326 ports. "sd-card" is written as "sd" to match PortKind.catalogConnector.

